 committed in their  presence or within their immediate  knowledge um that's the first paragraph  of that statute the second paragraph  states that if that offense is a felony  then the person has the right to um  prevent escape  of the offender um so what we have in  this case is the defense arguing that  they had knowledge or a reasonable cause  of suspicion basically probable cause to  believe that mr aubry had committed a  felony on a prior occasion and that they  were acting to prevent escape with that  felony on the day in question the  state's point is on the day in question  that mcmichael's had no knowledge  whatsoever of mr aubry committing  committing any offense let alone a  felony offense and that therefore they  had no right to try and effectuate a  citizen's arrest so the argument really  comes down to whether the jury believes  that the felony if one had been  committed had to have been in their  presence for immediate knowledge meaning  that day  or if they're entitled to take the  totality of the circumstances of their  all of their interactions with mr aubry  on prior occasions with a new from prior  videos that they saw on facebook page or  just neighborhood gossip whether they're  entitled to use that  for that reasonable and um  probable suspicion of a felony um that  he  they believe he was escaping from on the  day in question so for malice murder  they would have to show um  a  a malignant or abandoned abandoned and  malignant heart basically that they  acted with such depravity that it arises  to the level of  malice murder in georgia doesn't mean  what it may mean in other states there's  no premeditation required or anything of  that nature and of course the felony  murders mean that during the commission  of a felony um someone loses their life  and it was because of the commission of  that felony that caused the person to  lose their life in this case the  aggravated assaults being  um assaulting mr arbor with the trucks  from both the 