f mr arbury being a runner  and driving through the neighborhood and  that's what he was doing on this  occasion the defense needs the jury to  believe that mr albert was in the  neighborhood for  unlawful purposes that he was there  breaking into houses or casing a  property or something to that nature not  that he was just out for a job so the  purpose behind the comment i believe was  to paint a picture of someone in khaki  pants with you know shoes with no shock  socks um was not out for a daily job  i think there would have been there was  a better way to say that a less  inflammatory way up and do um grading  way of saying that  um  i don't believe it was a off-the-cuff  comment of looking at the defense's  argument i believe everything they said  was planned and very well thought out um  and as you said it may have been they  and and one thing about trial advocacy  is you have to know your audience or  believe you know your audience so  you know one has to wonder whether or  not um the attorneys believe that that  type of comment would have landed well  with that particular jury  and we won't know until they return to  birth yeah in our final minute together  melissa uh do you have any insight or  intel into what you think the jury may  be most considering in these  deliberations and where you potentially  see these deliberations landing  i believe a lot of there may be a  question and i think they will focus a  lot on the citizens of our statute and  that is because both sides argued it  very differently the defense argued that  the  felony that mr arbory could have been  fleeing from did not have to occur or  that mcmichaels didn't have to know he  had committed a felony that day they  could have used all their knowledge  and their belief that he had committed a  prior offense of burglary  that he was fleeing from on the day to  effectuate a citizen's arrest the state  argued that the statute means that the  felony has to occur either in your  presence or i